1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,640
A gardener has one strawberry plant, and wants

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:03,600
more.

3
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:05,280
There are two ways the plant can make

4
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:06,000
them.

5
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:08,080
It can send a runner along the ground,

6
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:10,320
and a new plant grows at the end.

7
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:12,560
Or it can flower.

8
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:14,560
Now picture a bee landing on one of

9
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:17,040
those flowers and brushing off a little yellow

10
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:19,120
dust, called pollen.

11
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:21,040
Has the plant made a seed yet?

12
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:22,000
Not yet.

13
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:24,080
The seed hasn't started.

14
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:26,240
So how does a flower make a seed,

15
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:27,760
and when is that a better route than

16
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:28,880
a runner?

17
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:30,720
By the end, you'll be able to outline

18
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:33,040
the whole journey in an exam answer.

19
00:00:34,267 --> 00:00:37,226
Here is a typical flower, cut in half.

20
00:00:37,467 --> 00:00:39,466
The plant has a problem.

21
00:00:39,547 --> 00:00:41,546
The cells that will become sperm are in

22
00:00:41,627 --> 00:00:43,946
one place, and its egg cells are somewhere

23
00:00:44,027 --> 00:00:45,066
else.

24
00:00:45,227 --> 00:00:47,466
Around the centre of the flower, the stamens

25
00:00:47,547 --> 00:00:51,066
hold the anthers, and the anthers make pollen.

26
00:00:51,227 --> 00:00:53,626
Each tiny pollen grain carries the cells that

27
00:00:53,707 --> 00:00:55,306
will become sperm.

28
00:00:55,387 --> 00:00:57,226
So the male side is up here.

29
00:00:58,434 --> 00:01:00,433
The female part is the carpel.

30
00:01:00,914 --> 00:01:03,473
Its sticky top is the stigma, and below

31
00:01:03,554 --> 00:01:06,673
it a narrow stalk, the style, leads down

32
00:01:06,754 --> 00:01:07,553
to the ovary.

33
00:01:08,194 --> 00:01:11,793
Inside the ovary are small structures called ovules,

34
00:01:11,874 --> 00:01:14,433
and each ovule holds an egg cell.

35
00:01:14,474 --> 00:01:16,593
So the egg is buried deep inside the

36
00:01:16,794 --> 00:01:17,633
flower.

37
00:01:17,674 --> 00:01:20,513
The sperm of a flowering plant can't swim,

38
00:01:20,674 --> 00:01:22,353
so how will they ever get there?

39
00:01:23,567 --> 00:01:25,566
The first step is pollination.

40
00:01:26,047 --> 00:01:28,526
Pollen moves from an anther to a stigma.

41
00:01:29,007 --> 00:01:31,646
Plants can't walk, so they use carriers.

42
00:01:32,127 --> 00:01:34,686
A bee visits for nectar, and pollen sticks

43
00:01:34,727 --> 00:01:35,726
to its hairy body.

44
00:01:36,287 --> 00:01:38,206
At the next flower, some of it rubs

45
00:01:38,287 --> 00:01:39,726
off onto the stigma.

46
00:01:40,047 --> 00:01:42,766
Grasses use the wind instead, and they release

47
00:01:42,847 --> 00:01:44,686
huge clouds of light pollen.

48
00:01:45,247 --> 00:01:46,846
Some water plants use water.

49
00:01:47,487 --> 00:01:49,326
But look at what has happened so far.

50
00:01:49,727 --> 00:01:51,726
The pollen has only arrived.

51
00:01:52,047 --> 00:01:54,206
The cells that will become sperm are still

52
00:01:54,247 --> 00:01:56,286
in the pollen grain, and the egg is

53
00:01:56,367 --> 00:01:57,566
still deep inside.

54
00:01:58,127 --> 00:02:00,686
So pollination is not fertilisation.

55
00:02:01,087 --> 00:02:02,766
It only puts the pollen in the right

56
00:02:02,847 --> 00:02:03,486
place.

57
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:07,740
What happens next takes place inside the flower.

58
00:02:08,220 --> 00:02:10,620
The pollen grain lands on the sticky stigma

59
00:02:10,700 --> 00:02:11,980
and takes in water.

60
00:02:12,460 --> 00:02:15,100
Then it grows a thin tube, the pollen

61
00:02:15,180 --> 00:02:18,300
tube, down through the style, guided by chemical

62
00:02:18,380 --> 00:02:19,900
signals from the ovule.

63
00:02:20,220 --> 00:02:22,700
The two sperm cells form inside the tube

64
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:24,860
as it grows, and they travel along it,

65
00:02:24,940 --> 00:02:26,700
so they are protected, and they don't need

66
00:02:26,780 --> 00:02:27,980
to swim.

67
00:02:28,060 --> 00:02:30,460
When the tube reaches the ovule, it grows

68
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:32,740
in through a small opening and releases the

69
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:35,580
sperm cells right beside the egg cell.

70
00:02:35,620 --> 00:02:38,460
So the tube solves the plant's distance problem.

71
00:02:38,580 --> 00:02:40,540
It carries the sperm to the egg.

72
00:02:41,767 --> 00:02:43,526
Now comes fertilisation.

73
00:02:44,007 --> 00:02:46,486
One sperm cell joins the egg cell.

74
00:02:46,727 --> 00:02:50,086
That joining makes one new cell, the zygote,

75
00:02:50,167 --> 00:02:52,086
the first cell of a new plant.

76
00:02:52,567 --> 00:02:54,086
Just as in the first video of this

77
00:02:54,327 --> 00:02:56,806
series, one set from the sperm plus one

78
00:02:56,887 --> 00:02:58,966
set from the egg make a full two

79
00:02:59,127 --> 00:02:59,606
sets.

80
00:03:00,087 --> 00:03:02,886
The zygote then divides again and again and

81
00:03:02,967 --> 00:03:05,446
grows into a tiny young plant, called an

82
00:03:05,527 --> 00:03:06,086
embryo.

83
00:03:06,647 --> 00:03:08,486
So this is the moment the new plant

84
00:03:08,727 --> 00:03:11,606
actually begins, and it happens only because the

85
00:03:11,767 --> 00:03:13,926
tube took the sperm to the egg.

86
00:03:15,134 --> 00:03:18,173
After fertilisation, the ovule changes.

87
00:03:18,574 --> 00:03:21,693
It becomes a seed: a protective package holding

88
00:03:21,774 --> 00:03:24,253
the embryo, a store of food and a

89
00:03:24,334 --> 00:03:25,893
tough outer coat.

90
00:03:26,254 --> 00:03:28,173
The food is there because the embryo has

91
00:03:28,254 --> 00:03:30,013
no leaves yet to make its own.

92
00:03:30,654 --> 00:03:33,293
In many plants, the ovary around the ovules

93
00:03:33,374 --> 00:03:35,853
grows into a fruit, like a pea pod,

94
00:03:35,894 --> 00:03:37,853
and it protects the seeds inside.

95
00:03:39,067 --> 00:03:42,066
Seeds are made for waiting and for travelling.

96
00:03:42,347 --> 00:03:45,146
The coat protects the embryo from drying out,

97
00:03:45,227 --> 00:03:48,106
and wind, water or animals can carry seeds

98
00:03:48,187 --> 00:03:49,306
away from the parent.

99
00:03:49,787 --> 00:03:52,586
That is seed dispersal, which is a different

100
00:03:52,667 --> 00:03:54,146
job from pollination.

101
00:03:54,507 --> 00:03:57,066
Later, when it has water, oxygen and a

102
00:03:57,147 --> 00:04:00,826
suitable temperature, the embryo germinates, which means it

103
00:04:00,907 --> 00:04:02,906
starts to grow roots and leaves.

104
00:04:04,134 --> 00:04:06,053
Plants have a second way of making new

105
00:04:06,134 --> 00:04:08,333
plants, with no flowers at all.

106
00:04:08,614 --> 00:04:10,613
A piece of the parent grows roots and

107
00:04:10,694 --> 00:04:12,973
leaves and becomes a separate plant.

108
00:04:13,254 --> 00:04:15,493
This is called vegetative propagation.

109
00:04:15,974 --> 00:04:17,933
You saw one example at the start, the

110
00:04:17,974 --> 00:04:18,853
strawberry runner.

111
00:04:19,494 --> 00:04:22,213
An onion bulb grows small new bulbs from

112
00:04:22,254 --> 00:04:23,653
the side of the parent bulb.

113
00:04:24,214 --> 00:04:26,693
A potato tuber sprouts new shoots from its

114
00:04:26,774 --> 00:04:27,253
eyes.

115
00:04:27,734 --> 00:04:29,813
And a gardener's cutting grows roots from the

116
00:04:29,974 --> 00:04:31,333
cut end of a stem.

117
00:04:31,654 --> 00:04:34,533
In every case, no sperm joined an egg.

118
00:04:35,014 --> 00:04:38,133
So this is asexual reproduction, and the new

119
00:04:38,214 --> 00:04:40,453
plant is a clone of the parent, apart

120
00:04:40,534 --> 00:04:41,813
from rare copying errors.

121
00:04:42,374 --> 00:04:44,293
It also doesn't need a bee or the

122
00:04:44,454 --> 00:04:45,973
wind to carry pollen.

123
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:50,000
So when is making seeds the better route?

124
00:04:50,240 --> 00:04:52,320
It depends on the conditions, just as in

125
00:04:52,400 --> 00:04:53,280
the first video.

126
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:56,240
In a steady paddock with plenty of water,

127
00:04:56,320 --> 00:04:58,480
a plant that is already doing well can

128
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:01,840
spread fast by runners, with no carrier needed.

129
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:04,400
But if a new disease arrives, a field

130
00:05:04,440 --> 00:05:07,280
of clones has the same weakness everywhere, and

131
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:08,960
the plants can fail together.

132
00:05:09,760 --> 00:05:12,880
Seeds come from sexual reproduction, so the seedlings

133
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:14,960
vary, and some may survive.

134
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:17,840
Seeds can travel far, so the plant can

135
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:19,040
reach new places.

136
00:05:19,680 --> 00:05:22,400
Runners are fast and keep the plant alike,

137
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:23,120
close by.

138
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:26,240
Seeds make varied plants, and they can travel

139
00:05:26,320 --> 00:05:26,880
far.

140
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:29,540
Let's build a model answer.

141
00:05:30,020 --> 00:05:34,020
The question: outline sexual reproduction in flowering plants

142
00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:36,340
from pollination to seed formation.

143
00:05:36,820 --> 00:05:39,780
The command word outline means give the main

144
00:05:39,860 --> 00:05:43,220
steps, in order, so we use sequence words.

145
00:05:43,700 --> 00:05:46,580
First, pollen is transferred from an anther to

146
00:05:46,620 --> 00:05:49,540
a stigma, by wind, water or animals.

147
00:05:50,180 --> 00:05:52,500
Then the pollen grain grows a pollen tube

148
00:05:52,660 --> 00:05:55,380
down the style to an ovule, carrying sperm

149
00:05:55,460 --> 00:05:55,940
cells.

150
00:05:56,580 --> 00:05:59,300
Next, a sperm cell joins the egg cell

151
00:05:59,380 --> 00:06:00,180
in the ovule.

152
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:03,620
That is fertilisation, and it forms a zygote

153
00:06:03,700 --> 00:06:05,580
that develops into an embryo.

154
00:06:05,940 --> 00:06:08,980
Finally, the ovule develops into a seed holding

155
00:06:09,060 --> 00:06:11,700
the embryo, and in many plants the ovary

156
00:06:11,780 --> 00:06:13,140
develops into a fruit.

157
00:06:14,367 --> 00:06:15,206
Your turn.

158
00:06:15,407 --> 00:06:16,606
Three situations.

159
00:06:17,087 --> 00:06:20,126
One: a grower covers some flowers with fine

160
00:06:20,247 --> 00:06:22,766
mesh bags before they open, so that bees

161
00:06:22,847 --> 00:06:24,286
and wind can't get to them.

162
00:06:24,847 --> 00:06:27,646
The grower also removes their anthers, so these

163
00:06:27,727 --> 00:06:29,646
flowers have no pollen of their own.

164
00:06:30,207 --> 00:06:32,486
Those flowers never make seeds.

165
00:06:32,767 --> 00:06:35,086
Which step failed, and why?

166
00:06:35,567 --> 00:06:38,686
Two: a gardener brushes pollen onto the stigma

167
00:06:38,727 --> 00:06:41,166
of a flower with a small paintbrush.

168
00:06:41,247 --> 00:06:43,726
Two days later, pollen tubes are growing down

169
00:06:43,807 --> 00:06:46,686
the style but have not reached the ovules.

170
00:06:46,767 --> 00:06:48,686
Has fertilisation happened yet?

171
00:06:48,767 --> 00:06:50,406
Why or why not?

172
00:06:50,847 --> 00:06:53,406
Three: a farmer grows a whole field of

173
00:06:53,487 --> 00:06:56,366
potatoes from pieces of last year's tubers, and

174
00:06:56,447 --> 00:06:58,366
a new disease arrives.

175
00:06:58,447 --> 00:07:00,446
Why is that field at more risk than

176
00:07:00,527 --> 00:07:02,366
a field grown from seed?

177
00:07:02,447 --> 00:07:04,286
Pause the video if you'd like more time.

178
00:07:14,700 --> 00:07:17,100
One, pollination failed.

179
00:07:17,340 --> 00:07:19,900
The mesh kept out pollen from other flowers,

180
00:07:19,980 --> 00:07:22,380
and with their anthers removed these flowers had

181
00:07:22,460 --> 00:07:25,020
no pollen of their own, so no pollen

182
00:07:25,060 --> 00:07:27,980
reached the stigmas, no pollen tube grew, no

183
00:07:28,060 --> 00:07:31,100
sperm met an egg, and no seeds formed.

184
00:07:31,180 --> 00:07:33,580
Fertilisation did not happen either, but that is

185
00:07:33,660 --> 00:07:34,940
a knock-on effect.

186
00:07:35,020 --> 00:07:37,980
The first step to fail was pollination.

187
00:07:38,140 --> 00:07:39,900
Two, not yet.

188
00:07:39,980 --> 00:07:41,980
The brush did the pollination, and the tubes

189
00:07:42,060 --> 00:07:43,500
are still on their way.

190
00:07:43,540 --> 00:07:46,220
Fertilisation happens when a sperm joins an egg

191
00:07:46,260 --> 00:07:47,420
in the ovule.

192
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:49,260
If you said yes because the pollen is

193
00:07:49,460 --> 00:07:53,820
already on the flower, that's pollination, not fertilisation.

194
00:07:54,020 --> 00:07:56,780
Three, the potatoes are all clones, so they

195
00:07:56,860 --> 00:07:58,460
share the same alleles.

196
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:00,540
A disease that can attack one can attack

197
00:08:00,620 --> 00:08:01,500
them all.

198
00:08:01,580 --> 00:08:05,060
Seedlings from seed vary, so some might survive.

199
00:08:05,420 --> 00:08:08,060
If you said clones grow worse, they do

200
00:08:08,220 --> 00:08:10,620
just as well until something changes.

201
00:08:11,834 --> 00:08:13,673
So, hold on to one rule.

202
00:08:13,754 --> 00:08:16,633
Pollination is the pollen arriving.

203
00:08:17,114 --> 00:08:19,993
Fertilisation is the sperm joining the egg.

204
00:08:20,474 --> 00:08:23,553
In between, the pollen tube does the carrying.

205
00:08:23,834 --> 00:08:27,833
In order: land, tube, join, seed.

206
00:08:28,314 --> 00:08:30,953
And to keep the parts straight, stamen has

207
00:08:31,114 --> 00:08:33,353
"men" in it, so the stamen is the

208
00:08:33,434 --> 00:08:34,233
male part.

209
00:08:35,034 --> 00:08:39,033
Next: how single-celled organisms make copies of themselves.

210
00:08:40,234 --> 00:08:41,753
Here are the key notes.

211
00:08:41,994 --> 00:08:43,913
Pause the video if you need more time

212
00:08:43,994 --> 00:08:44,793
to copy them.
